> Module Dependents_benefit_split

> Using Qualifying_child as Q

# Children of divorced or separated parents: IRC 152(e) and Form 8332

Direct File ("DF" below) module `dependentsBenefitSplit.xml` (687 lines, under
`../direct-file/direct-file/backend/src/main/resources/tax/`), every fact.
Issue #18, scope.md section 5.5. Tests:
`catala/tests/test_dependents_benefit_split.catala_en`. Oracle comparison:
`difftest/mappings/dependents_benefit_split.mjs` against the per-person oracle
`oracle/persons.csv` (`make difftest-persons`).

DF calls the rule of IRC 152(e) a "benefit split": when the parents of a child
are divorced, separated or live apart and the custodial parent signs a written
declaration (Form 8332) not to claim the child, the noncustodial parent claims
the child as a dependent and for the child tax credit, the additional child
tax credit and the credit for other dependents, while the custodial parent
keeps head of household, the earned income credit and the child and dependent
care credit. DF also lets a filer who is not a parent (a grandparent, an aunt)
use the child for those three benefits when the custodial parent does not.

Like `Qualifying_child` and `Qualifying_relative`, this module works on one
member of the DF collection `/familyAndHousehold` at a time (scope
`BenefitSplitTest`) and maps that scope over the household (scope
`BenefitSplit`). It calls `Qualifying_child.QualifyingChildTest` for the same
person, for the age on July 2, the disability gate and the relationship test.
It does not read the qualifying relative or dependent tests, so
`Qualifying_relative` can read this module's outputs without a cycle.

Facts read from outside the person, all inputs here:

- `/minimumFilerDateOfBirth` (`Filers.minimum_filer_date_of_birth`, #15), for
  the qualifying child scope;
- `/treatAsMFJ` (`filingStatus.xml`; `Filers.treat_as_mfj`, #15), read only by
  the two DF display facts `primaryFilerNotClaiming` and
  `bothFilersNotClaiming`.

The outputs read elsewhere in DF (`Export downstreamFacts`):
`eligibleForBenefitSplit`, `tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc`,
`disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody` (all in `familyAndHousehold.xml`: dependent,
HoH, QSS, EITC and CDCC person tests) and the two knockouts
`knockedOutByContradictory8832` and `knockedOutByNonCustodialParentBenefitSplit`
(`flow.xml` lines 68-69). This module feeds no Form 1040 line directly.

The inputs are complete (CONVENTIONS section 3). DF asks most of the questions
below only on one path (parent or non-parent) and only after the earlier
answers allow the split. They are still inputs here; every rule below is the
DF rule, which already ignores an answer off its path, except where marked
`# DF-BUG(18)` (the multiple support agreement exception, IRC 152(e)(5)).

Not here, on purpose: a noncustodial parent claiming the child (a DF
knockout, below), the pre-1985 decrees of IRC 152(e)(3) (see section (3)
below), the tie-breakers of IRC 152(c)(4) between a parent and a non-parent
when no declaration was signed (DF-BUG-3, `Qualifying_child`), and the
credit-specific person tests that read the outputs (issues #19, #26-#28).

## Declarations

```catala-metadata
## DF: dependentsBenefitSplit.xml /parentalSituationOptions (lines 4-14), same
## order. The filer's "marital or living situation with the other biological
## or adoptive parent at the end of" the tax year. DF's help text
## (df-client-app/src/locales/en.yaml line 15028): "If you're divorced or
## legally separated under a decree of divorce or separate maintenance, or
## separated under a written separation agreement, answer Divorced or
## separated", so `DivorcedOrSeparated` is IRC 152(e)(1)(A)(i) or (ii).
declaration enumeration ParentalSituation:
  -- DivorcedOrSeparated
  -- LivedApartLastSixMonths
  -- Other

## DF: dependentsBenefitSplit.xml /writtenDeclarationOptions (lines 16-28),
## same order. "Did one of <child>'s parents sign a form 8332, or other
## written declaration, not to claim <child> as a dependent?" `IDid`: the
## filer (on a joint return, either spouse) signed; `TheyDid`: the child's
## other biological or adoptive parent signed.
declaration enumeration WrittenDeclaration:
  -- IDid
  -- TheyDid
  -- NobodyDid

## DF: constants.xml /moreLessEqualOptions (lines 774-784), same order.
## "Did <child> live more nights of the year with you or the other parent?"
declaration enumeration NightsComparison:
  -- More
  -- Less
  -- Equal

## The `Writable` answers DF keeps per household member that the benefit
## split reads, beyond those of `Qualifying_child.Person`.
declaration structure BenefitSplitAnswers:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hasOtherBiologicalOrAdoptiveParent ("Does the
  # dependent have another legal parent apart from the taxpayer and spouse?")
  data has_other_biological_or_adoptive_parent content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/nonParentalSomeParentNotClaiming (non-parent
  # path: "Has any of the dependent's parents signed a written declaration
  # not to claim them?")
  data non_parental_some_parent_not_claiming content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/whichParentNotClaiming (parent path)
  data which_parent_not_claiming content WrittenDeclaration
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/inParentsCustody ("As of July 2, was the
  # dependent still in the legal custody of their biological or adoptive
  # parents?")
  data in_parents_custody content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/livedWithTpOrOtherBiologicalOrAdoptiveParentMoreThanSixMonths
  data lived_with_tp_or_other_biological_or_adoptive_parent_more_than_six_months
    content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/biologicalOrAdoptiveParentsLiving
  data biological_or_adoptive_parents_living content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/nightsWithTpVsOtherParent
  data nights_with_tp_vs_other_parent content NightsComparison
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpAgiHigherThanOtherParent. An answer, not a
  # comparison of two AGIs: the filer's AGI is computed only at stage 4
  # (CONVENTIONS section 7) and the other parent's AGI is not on this return.
  data tp_agi_higher_than_other_parent content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/parentsMeetReqsRuleForChildrenOfDivorcedParents
  # (non-parent path: the parents meet "the requirements of the rule for
  # children of divorced or separated parents (or parents who live apart)")
  data parents_meet_reqs_rule_for_children_of_divorced_parents content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/specialRuleChildUsedByCustodialParentAsQP
  # (non-parent path: the custodial parent uses the child for HoH, EITC or
  # CDCC)
  data special_rule_child_used_by_custodial_parent_as_qp content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/specialRuleAgiTest (non-parent path: the
  # filer's AGI is higher than the custodial parent's)
  data special_rule_agi_test content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/parentalSituation
  data parental_situation content ParentalSituation
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/parentalSupport ("Did you and/or the other
  # biological or adoptive parent pay for more than half of <child>'s total
  # support?")
  data parental_support content boolean
  # No DF fact (DF-BUG(18)): over one-half of the child's support is treated
  # as received from a taxpayer under a multiple support agreement (IRC
  # 152(d)(3), Form 2120). DF never asks; its value is false for every DF
  # return.
  data multiple_support_agreement content boolean

## One member of DF /familyAndHousehold: the qualifying-child answers and the
## benefit-split answers.
declaration structure Member:
  data person content Q.Person
  data answers content BenefitSplitAnswers

## Age of majority. Custody is a matter of state law (Treas. Reg.
## 1.152-4(c)); DF assumes custody ends at 18 (description of
## /familyAndHousehold/*/childMayQualifyForBenefitSplit). True in every state
## DF supports (Types.ScopedState: no AL, NE (19), MS or PR (21)). Out of
## scope per issue #18; flagged as a candidate in the PR.
declaration age_of_majority content integer equals 18
```

## The scope for one person

```catala-metadata
declaration scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # One member of DF /familyAndHousehold
  input member content Member
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth (Filers.minimum_filer_date_of_birth)
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date
  # DF: filingStatus.xml /treatAsMFJ (Filers.treat_as_mfj)
  input treat_as_mfj content boolean

  # The qualifying child tests for the same person (issue #16)
  output qc content Q.QualifyingChildTest

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/childMayQualifyForBenefitSplit
  output child_may_qualify_for_benefit_split condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpIsParent
  output tp_is_parent condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpIsNotParent
  output tp_is_not_parent condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/parentalSomeParentNotClaiming
  output parental_some_parent_not_claiming condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/primaryFilerNotClaiming
  output primary_filer_not_claiming condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/bothFilersNotClaiming
  output both_filers_not_claiming condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/otherParentNotClaiming
  output other_parent_not_claiming condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hadEqualNumberOfNightsWithParents
  output had_equal_number_of_nights_with_parents condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/parentsAreDivorcedSeparatedOrLivingApart
  output parents_are_divorced_separated_or_living_apart condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpIsCustodialParent
  output tp_is_custodial_parent condition
  # IRC 152(e)(5) (no DF fact; DF-BUG(18))
  output section_152e_applies_despite_support_agreements condition
  # The two branches of DF's /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleForBenefitSplit
  # (no DF fact each; kept apart so each branch sits under its own law)
  internal eligible_on_parental_path condition
  internal eligible_on_non_parental_path condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleForBenefitSplit
  output eligible_for_benefit_split condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/reachedNonParentalBenefitSplit
  output reached_non_parental_benefit_split condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc
  output tp_can_claim_split_benefits_hoh_eitc_cdcc condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody
  output disqualified_due_to_parental_custody condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/contradictory8832TPIsCustodialParent
  output contradictory8832_tp_is_custodial_parent condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/contradictory8832TPNotCustodialParent
  output contradictory8832_tp_not_custodial_parent condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/contradictory8832Knockout
  output contradictory8832_knockout condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/causesNoncustodialParentBenefitSplitKnockout
  output causes_noncustodial_parent_benefit_split_knockout condition
  # This person makes the return unsupported (either knockout above)
  output unsupported condition
```

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  definition qc equals
    output of Q.QualifyingChildTest with {
      -- person: member.person
      -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: minimum_filer_date_of_birth }
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 152(e) Special rule for divorced parents, etc.

### (1) In general

IRC 152(e)(1) (`sources/statute/irc-152.html`): "Notwithstanding subsection
(c)(1)(B), (c)(4), or (d)(1)(C), if— (A) a child receives over one-half of the
child's support during the calendar year from the child's parents— (i) who
are divorced or legally separated under a decree of divorce or separate
maintenance, (ii) who are separated under a written separation agreement, or
(iii) who live apart at all times during the last 6 months of the calendar
year, and— (B) such child is in the custody of 1 or both of the child's
parents for more than one-half of the calendar year, such child shall be
treated as being the qualifying child or qualifying relative of the
noncustodial parent for a calendar year if the requirements described in
paragraph (2) or (3) are met."

Pub. 501 (2024), `sources/pubs/p501--2024.pdf` p 13, "Children of divorced or
separated parents (or parents who live apart)": "the child will be treated as
the qualifying child of the noncustodial parent if all four of the following
statements are true. 1. The parents: a. Are divorced or legally separated
under a decree of divorce or separate maintenance; b. Are separated under a
written separation agreement; or c. Lived apart at all times during the last
6 months of the year, whether or not they are or were married. 2. The child
received over half of the child's support for the year from the parents. 3.
The child is in the custody of one or both parents for more than half of the
year. 4. Either of the following statements is true. a. The custodial parent
signs a written declaration, discussed later, that they won't claim the child
as a dependent for the year, and the noncustodial parent attaches this
written declaration to their return. ... b. A pre-1985 decree of divorce or
separate maintenance or written separation agreement ..."

Form 8332 (Rev. December 2025), `sources/forms-instructions/f8332.pdf` p 1,
"Special Rule for Children of Divorced or Separated Parents" says the same
(no 2024 revision exists, `sources/INDEX.md`; the text quoted here is the
statute's and Pub. 501's, which are 2024).

#### (B) Custody for more than one-half of the year

Treas. Reg. § 1.152-4(c) (`sources/regs/cfr-1.152-4.xml`): "A child is in the
custody of one or both parents for more than one-half of the calendar year if
one or both parents have the right under state law to physical custody of the
child for more than one-half of the calendar year." § 1.152-4(d)(1): "A child
is treated as residing with neither parent if the child is emancipated under
state law." Pub. 501 p 14, "Example 5—child emancipated in May. Your child
turned 18 in May 2024 and became emancipated under the law of the state where
your child lives. As a result, your child isn't considered in the custody of
either parent for more than half of the year."

DF first screens on age: `childMayQualifyForBenefitSplit` is true if the
person is under 18 on July 2 (`ageAtFirstHalfOfYear`, the DF day that splits
2024's 366 days into 183 + 183) or is permanently and totally disabled, then
asks about legal custody on July 2 (`inParentsCustody`) and about living with
a parent for more than six months. A child who turns 18 (`age_of_majority`)
on or before July 2 is in custody at most 183 of 366 days, not "more than
one-half"; one who turns 18 on July 3 is in custody 184 days. So the screen is
exact when custody ends at 18.

DF writes the disability half as `All(disabilityStatusMayAffectBenefits,
permanentTotalDisability)`. The gate is true for everyone 18 or older on July
2, and the first half of the `Any` already covers everyone younger, so the
value is the same as "under 18 or disabled" for every input; we keep DF's
form.

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(1)(B); Treas. Reg. 1.152-4(c), (d)(1); Pub 501 p 14 Example 5.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/childMayQualifyForBenefitSplit
  rule child_may_qualify_for_benefit_split under condition
    qc.age_at_first_half_of_year < age_of_majority
    or (qc.disability_status_may_affect_benefits
        and member.person.permanent_total_disability)
  consequence fulfilled
```

#### Who is a parent

The rule is about "the child's parents". DF's parent path is for a filer whose
relationship to the person is `biologicalChild` or `adoptedChild` (IRC
152(f)(1)(B): "a legally adopted individual of the taxpayer ... shall be
treated as a child of such individual by blood"); the questions name "the
other biological or adoptive parent". Every other relationship that passes
the qualifying child relationship test (a stepparent, a grandparent, a
sibling, a foster parent) takes the non-parent path.

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(1) "the child's parents", 152(f)(1)(B).
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpIsParent
  rule tp_is_parent under condition
    member.person.relationship = Q.Relationship.BiologicalChild
    or member.person.relationship = Q.Relationship.AdoptedChild
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(c)(2). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpIsNotParent
  rule tp_is_not_parent under condition
    not tp_is_parent and qc.qc_relationship_test
  consequence fulfilled
```

#### (A)(i)-(iii) Divorced, separated, or living apart

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(1)(A)(i)-(iii); Pub 501 p 13 statement 1, p 14 "Parents who
  # never married". DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/parentsAreDivorcedSeparatedOrLivingApart
  rule parents_are_divorced_separated_or_living_apart under condition
    member.answers.parental_situation != Other
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (2) Exception where custodial parent releases claim to exemption

IRC 152(e)(2): "the requirements described in this paragraph are met with
respect to any calendar year if— (A) the custodial parent signs a written
declaration (in such manner and form as the Secretary may by regulations
prescribe) that such custodial parent will not claim such child as a
dependent for any taxable year beginning in such calendar year, and (B) the
noncustodial parent attaches such written declaration to the noncustodial
parent's return for the taxable year beginning during such calendar year."

Treas. Reg. § 1.152-4(e)(1)(ii): "A written declaration may be made on Form
8332, Release/Revocation of Release of Claim to Exemption for Child by
Custodial Parent, or successor form designated by the IRS." Form 8332 Part I:
"I agree not to claim an exemption for [name of child] for the tax year 20__.
[Signature of custodial parent releasing claim to exemption]".

DF asks the parent which parent signed (`whichParentNotClaiming`). The three
facts below name its answers; `primaryFilerNotClaiming` and
`bothFilersNotClaiming` split `iDid` by `/treatAsMFJ` and are read by no other
fact (they choose screen text).

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(2)(A). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/parentalSomeParentNotClaiming
  rule parental_some_parent_not_claiming under condition
    member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = IDid
    or member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = TheyDid
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/primaryFilerNotClaiming
  rule primary_filer_not_claiming under condition
    member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = IDid and not treat_as_mfj
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/bothFilersNotClaiming
  rule both_filers_not_claiming under condition
    member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = IDid and treat_as_mfj
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/otherParentNotClaiming
  rule other_parent_not_claiming under condition
    member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = TheyDid
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (3) Exception for certain pre-1985 instruments

IRC 152(e)(3)(A): "the requirements described in this paragraph are met with
respect to any calendar year if— (i) a qualified pre-1985 instrument between
the parents applicable to the taxable year beginning in such calendar year
provides that the noncustodial parent shall be entitled to any deduction
allowable under section 151 for such child, and (ii) the noncustodial parent
provides at least $600 for the support of such child during such calendar
year." A "qualified pre-1985 instrument" is one "executed before January 1,
1985" (152(e)(3)(B)(i)).

Not modelled; DF does not ask. Only a noncustodial parent can rely on it, and
DF knocks out every noncustodial parent (below), so it changes no supported
return. Noted in the PR.

### (4) Custodial parent and noncustodial parent

IRC 152(e)(4): "(A) Custodial parent. The term "custodial parent" means the
parent having custody for the greater portion of the calendar year. (B)
Noncustodial parent. The term "noncustodial parent" means the parent who is
not the custodial parent."

Treas. Reg. § 1.152-4(d)(1): "The custodial parent is the parent with whom the
child resides for the greater number of nights during the calendar year, and
the noncustodial parent is the parent who is not the custodial parent."
§ 1.152-4(d)(4): "If a child is in the custody of one or both parents for more
than one-half of the calendar year and the child resides with each parent for
an equal number of nights during the calendar year, the parent with the
higher adjusted gross income for the calendar year is treated as the
custodial parent."

Pub. 501 p 14: "Custodial parent and noncustodial parent. The custodial
parent is the parent with whom the child lived for the greater number of
nights during the year. The other parent is the noncustodial parent." "Equal
number of nights. If the child lived with each parent for an equal number of
nights during the year, the custodial parent is the parent with the higher
AGI." Example 1: "your child lived with you 210 nights and with the other
parent 156 nights. You are the custodial parent." Example 3: "Your child
lived with you 180 nights during the year and lived the same number of nights
with the child's other parent, your ex-spouse. Your AGI is $40,000. Your
ex-spouse's AGI is $25,000. You are treated as your child's custodial parent
because you have the higher AGI."

The other night-counting rules of § 1.152-4(d)(1)-(5) (nights away, the night
of December 31, emancipation, a parent who works at night) decide how the
nights are counted, and DF shows them as help text with the nights question
("Who is the custodial parent?", `en.yaml` lines 13110-13150, including
"Parent works at night ... that parent is treated as the custodial parent").
So the `nights_with_tp_vs_other_parent` answer is the comparison after those
rules. With equal nights and equal AGIs neither AGI is "higher" and the filer
is not the custodial parent, as in DF.

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # Treas. Reg. 1.152-4(d)(4). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hadEqualNumberOfNightsWithParents
  rule had_equal_number_of_nights_with_parents under condition
    member.answers.nights_with_tp_vs_other_parent = Equal
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(e)(4)(A); Treas. Reg. 1.152-4(d)(1), (4); Pub 501 p 14,
  # Examples 1 and 3. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpIsCustodialParent
  rule tp_is_custodial_parent under condition
    member.answers.nights_with_tp_vs_other_parent = More
    or (member.answers.nights_with_tp_vs_other_parent = Equal
        and member.answers.tp_agi_higher_than_other_parent)
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (5) Exception for multiple-support agreement

IRC 152(e)(5): "This subsection shall not apply in any case where over
one-half of the support of the child is treated as having been received from
a taxpayer under the provision of subsection (d)(3)." Treas. Reg.
§ 1.152-4(b)(2)(ii): "The requirements of this paragraph (b)(2) are not met if
over one-half of the support of the child is treated as having been received
from a taxpayer under section 152(d)(3)." Form 8332 p 2: "Exception. If the
support of the child is determined under a multiple support agreement, this
special rule does not apply, and this form should not be used."

DF has no multiple support agreement anywhere (no fact mentions one). Such an
agreement is possible here: the parents together may pay over half of the
support while neither pays over half alone (152(d)(3)(A), "no one person
contributed over one-half"), and the agreement then names someone else
(another relative) as the person who claims. DF would still apply the split.
We follow the statute with the extra input
`multiple_support_agreement`; it is false on every DF return, so no oracle
value changes.

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # DF-BUG(18): DF never asks about a multiple support agreement and applies
  # the benefit split regardless. IRC 152(e)(5); Treas. Reg. 1.152-4(b)(2)(ii);
  # Form 8332 p 2 "Exception". Status: candidate. No oracle value changes.
  # IRC 152(e)(5) (no DF fact)
  rule section_152e_applies_despite_support_agreements under condition
    not member.answers.multiple_support_agreement
  consequence fulfilled
```

### The filer is a parent: eligible for the split

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleForBenefitSplit` (line 616), parent path:
the person may qualify (custody age), the filer is a parent, there is another
parent, one of the two signed a declaration (statement 4), the child was in
the parents' legal custody on July 2 and lived with them for more than six
months (statement 3), the parents are divorced, separated or live apart
(statement 1), and the parents paid over half of the support (statement 2).

The non-parent path of the same fact is in the next section.

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(1), (2), (5); Pub 501 p 13 statements 1-4.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleForBenefitSplit, first branch
  rule eligible_on_parental_path under condition
    child_may_qualify_for_benefit_split
    and tp_is_parent
    and member.answers.has_other_biological_or_adoptive_parent
    and parental_some_parent_not_claiming
    and member.answers.in_parents_custody
    and member.answers.lived_with_tp_or_other_biological_or_adoptive_parent_more_than_six_months
    and parents_are_divorced_separated_or_living_apart
    and member.answers.parental_support
    and section_152e_applies_despite_support_agreements
  consequence fulfilled
```

### The filer is not a parent

When 152(e) applies, the child is "the qualifying child or qualifying relative
of the noncustodial parent ... Notwithstanding subsection ... (c)(4)", so no
one else claims the child as a dependent. HoH and the EITC read the
qualifying child "determined without regard to section 152(e)" (IRC
2(b)(1)(A)(i), 32(c)(3)(A)), so for them the child stays a qualifying child of
everyone who passes 152(c), and the tie-breakers of 152(c)(4) decide.
152(c)(4)(C): "if the parents of an individual may claim such individual as a
qualifying child but no parent so claims the individual, such individual may
be claimed as the qualifying child of another taxpayer but only if the
adjusted gross income of such taxpayer is higher than the highest adjusted
gross income of any parent of the individual."

Pub. 501 pp 17-18, "Applying the tiebreaker rules to divorced or separated
parents (or parents who live apart)", Example 1: Kody, 5, lives with you and
your parent; Kody's other parent lives elsewhere. "Kody is treated as the
qualifying child of Kody's other parent, who can claim the child tax credit
for the child. ... However, you agree to let your parent claim Kody. This
means your parent can claim Kody for head of household filing status and the
earned income credit if your parent qualifies for each and if you don't claim
Kody as a qualifying child for the earned income credit." Example 2: "Your
parent can't claim Kody as a qualifying child for any purpose because your
parent's AGI isn't higher than yours."

DF reaches the non-parent split (`reachedNonParentalBenefitSplit`, line 646)
when the person may qualify, the filer is not a parent but passes the
relationship test, the parents are living, the child was in their custody on
July 2, one parent signed a declaration, and the parents meet the rule (a
single question for statements 1-3). Then the filer may use the child for
HoH, EITC and CDCC (`eligibleForBenefitSplit`, non-parent path) if the
custodial parent does not use the child for those benefits and the filer's
AGI is higher (Examples 1 and 2); otherwise the person is
`disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody` and gives the filer no benefit at all.

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(1), (5); Pub 501 pp 17-18.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/reachedNonParentalBenefitSplit
  rule reached_non_parental_benefit_split under condition
    child_may_qualify_for_benefit_split
    and tp_is_not_parent
    and member.answers.biological_or_adoptive_parents_living
    and member.answers.in_parents_custody
    and member.answers.non_parental_some_parent_not_claiming
    and member.answers.parents_meet_reqs_rule_for_children_of_divorced_parents
    and section_152e_applies_despite_support_agreements
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(c)(4)(C); Pub 501 pp 17-18, Examples 1 and 2.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleForBenefitSplit, second branch
  rule eligible_on_non_parental_path under condition
    reached_non_parental_benefit_split
    and not member.answers.special_rule_child_used_by_custodial_parent_as_qp
    and member.answers.special_rule_agi_test
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(e)(1); 152(c)(4)(C). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleForBenefitSplit
  rule eligible_for_benefit_split under condition
    eligible_on_parental_path or eligible_on_non_parental_path
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(e)(1) (the noncustodial parent claims the dependent),
  # 152(c)(4)(A), (C); Pub 501 pp 17-18, Examples 2 and 3.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody
  rule disqualified_due_to_parental_custody under condition
    reached_non_parental_benefit_split
    and (not member.answers.special_rule_agi_test
         or member.answers.special_rule_child_used_by_custodial_parent_as_qp)
  consequence fulfilled
```

## What the custodial parent keeps

Pub. 501 p 13: "If statements (1) through (4) are all true, only the
noncustodial parent can: • Claim the child as a dependent; and • Claim the
child as a qualifying child for the child tax credit, the credit for other
dependents, or the additional child tax credit. However, this doesn't allow
the noncustodial parent to claim head of household filing status, the credit
for child and dependent care expenses, the exclusion for dependent care
benefits, or the earned income credit." Pub. 501 p 10, HoH table note 1: "If
you are the custodial parent and those rules apply, the child is generally
your qualifying child for head of household filing status even though the
child isn't a qualifying child you can claim as a dependent."

The statute: IRC 2(b)(1)(A)(i), HoH, "a qualifying child of the individual
(as defined in section 152(c), determined without regard to section 152(e))";
IRC 32(c)(3)(A), EITC, "a qualifying child of the taxpayer (as defined in
section 152(c), determined without regard to paragraph (1)(D) thereof and
section 152(e))"; IRC 21(e)(5), CDCC: "If— (A) section 152(e) applies to any
child with respect to any calendar year, and (B) such child is under the age
of 13 or is physically or mentally incapable of caring for himself, ... such
child shall be treated as a qualifying individual ... with respect to the
custodial parent (as defined in section 152(e)(4)(A)), and shall not be
treated as a qualifying individual with respect to the noncustodial parent."

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc` (line 388): the
person is eligible for the split, and either the filer is the custodial parent
who signed (`iDid`), or the filer is a non-parent who reached the split.
Readers (`familyAndHousehold.xml`): `eligibleDependentMinusFilerInformation`
(not a dependent, so no CTC, ACTC or ODC), `hohQualifyingPerson`,
`eitcQualifyingChild`, `cdccNonDependentQualifyingPerson`,
`qssQualifyingPerson` and `askAboutQCOfAnother`. If the other parent signed
and the filer is the custodial parent, there is no split and the filer keeps
everything (DF's comment, line 410), but DF knocks that answer out as
contradictory (below).

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(1), (2); IRC 2(b)(1)(A)(i), 32(c)(3)(A), 21(e)(5); Pub 501 pp
  # 10, 13. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc
  rule tp_can_claim_split_benefits_hoh_eitc_cdcc under condition
    eligible_for_benefit_split
    and ((member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = IDid
          and tp_is_custodial_parent)
         or reached_non_parental_benefit_split)
  consequence fulfilled
```

## Knockouts

### Contradictory Form 8332 answers

Only the custodial parent signs the release (IRC 152(e)(2)(A), "the custodial
parent signs a written declaration"; Form 8332 Part I, "Signature of
custodial parent releasing claim to exemption"). A filer who is the custodial
parent by the nights test but says the other parent signed, or who is not the
custodial parent but says they signed, contradicts that. DF knocks the return
out (`contradictory8832Knockout`, line 459; its description cites an internal
issue). Treas. Reg. § 1.152-4(e)(4): "A written declaration ... that fails to
satisfy the requirements of this paragraph (e) has no effect", so the law
would ignore the declaration rather than refuse the return; refusing is a
scope decision, not a computation, and is reproduced (CONVENTIONS section 6).

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(2)(A). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/contradictory8832TPIsCustodialParent
  rule contradictory8832_tp_is_custodial_parent under condition
    tp_is_custodial_parent
    and member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = TheyDid
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(e)(2)(A). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/contradictory8832TPNotCustodialParent
  rule contradictory8832_tp_not_custodial_parent under condition
    not tp_is_custodial_parent
    and member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = IDid
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/contradictory8832Knockout
  rule contradictory8832_knockout under condition
    eligible_for_benefit_split
    and (contradictory8832_tp_is_custodial_parent
         or contradictory8832_tp_not_custodial_parent)
  consequence fulfilled
```

### The filer is the noncustodial parent

The noncustodial parent who holds a release claims the child as a dependent
and for the CTC, ACTC and ODC (IRC 152(e)(1); Pub. 501 p 13), and must attach
Form 8332 (152(e)(2)(B)). DF does not support that return
(`causesNoncustodialParentBenefitSplitKnockout`, line 552): the person is
eligible for the split, the filer is not the custodial parent, and the other
parent signed. scope.md section 5.5: "Noncustodial claimants and
contradictory 8332 answers are knockouts."

```catala
scope BenefitSplitTest:
  # IRC 152(e)(1), (2)(B); scope.md 5.5.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/causesNoncustodialParentBenefitSplitKnockout
  rule causes_noncustodial_parent_benefit_split_knockout under condition
    eligible_for_benefit_split
    and not tp_is_custodial_parent
    and member.answers.which_parent_not_claiming = TheyDid
  consequence fulfilled

  # CONVENTIONS section 6 (no DF fact; the per-person part of the two
  # flow.xml knockouts below)
  rule unsupported under condition
    contradictory8832_knockout
    or causes_noncustodial_parent_benefit_split_knockout
  consequence fulfilled
```

## The household: every person

The scope `BenefitSplit` applies `BenefitSplitTest` to each member of
`/familyAndHousehold`, in order. DF's two return-level knockouts
(`/knockedOutByContradictory8832`, line 513, and
`/knockedOutByNonCustodialParentBenefitSplit`, line 575, read by `flow.xml`
lines 68-69 into `/flowIsKnockedOut`) are "the count of persons with the
per-person knockout is greater than 0". `/contradictory8832TPIsCustodialParentTriggeredKnockout`
and `/contradictory8832TPNotCustodialParentTriggeredKnockout`
(lines 479 and 496) count the two contradiction facts without the
`eligibleForBenefitSplit` condition; they choose the knockout screen's text and are read by no other
fact, so they are not modelled. Neither are the two `dependentWhoCaused...`
facts (lines 533 and 598), which name the person for the screen.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope BenefitSplit:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold
  input members content list of Member
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date
  # DF: filingStatus.xml /treatAsMFJ
  input treat_as_mfj content boolean

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/... (one result per member, same order)
  output results content list of BenefitSplitTest
  # DF: /knockedOutByContradictory8832
  output ko_knocked_out_by_contradictory8832 condition
  # DF: /knockedOutByNonCustodialParentBenefitSplit
  output ko_knocked_out_by_non_custodial_parent_benefit_split condition
  # CONVENTIONS section 6: every ko_ of this scope
  output unsupported condition
```

```catala
scope BenefitSplit:
  definition results equals
    map each m among members to
      (output of BenefitSplitTest with {
         -- member: m
         -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: minimum_filer_date_of_birth
         -- treat_as_mfj: treat_as_mfj })

  # DF: /knockedOutByContradictory8832
  rule ko_knocked_out_by_contradictory8832 under condition
    exists r among results such that r.contradictory8832_knockout
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /knockedOutByNonCustodialParentBenefitSplit
  rule ko_knocked_out_by_non_custodial_parent_benefit_split under condition
    exists r among results such that
      r.causes_noncustodial_parent_benefit_split_knockout
  consequence fulfilled

  # flow.xml lines 68-69
  rule unsupported under condition
    ko_knocked_out_by_contradictory8832
    or ko_knocked_out_by_non_custodial_parent_benefit_split
  consequence fulfilled
```
